  "- **2026-09-25 MP4-to-OGV staged-save rejection:** The hidden route reused the bounded M2V staged-save topology on the byte-identical 147,242,147-byte H.264 MP4 from the earlier rejection. Both cold Chrome runs genuinely decoded and re-encoded the same 8,840,421-byte Ogg/Theora file with the original SHA-256, 1,560 packets, full native decode, bounded 256 KiB I/O/queueing, one pending write, and 32 MiB Wasm. One session measured 240.520 MiB complete-process-tree incremental private memory, but the second measured **264.445 MiB** against the unchanged 250 MiB ceiling. Their absolute peaks were almost identical near 507 MiB; the lower stable blank baseline in the second session revealed the failure. The staged path also took about 34 seconds instead of the older roughly 22-second candidate. A final-copy cancellation checkpoint raced with the small 8.8 MB output and was corrected to early-encode cancellation, which passed cleanup; neither result justified keeping this slower, nonrepeatable candidate. MP4-specific staging was reverted, the route remains hidden/failed, and the selective fixture-generator option remains to avoid creating an unrelated MOV. Exact report hashes and cleanup are in `evidence/mp4-to-ogv-staged-save-rejection-2026-09-25.json`.",
  "- **2026-09-26 bounded structured-source inspection:** The pre-conversion panel now reads at most 256 KiB and reports non-content structure for CSV, TSV, NDJSON, JSON, XML, TXT, Markdown, HTML, SRT, WebVTT, ASS/SSA, TTML, and SVG. It shows record/line/cue counts, header width, top-level JSON shape, root/DOCTYPE signals, or subtitle sections; truncated-prefix counts are explicitly lower bounds. The existing privacy conversion test and focused JSON/HTML/SRT gate passed 2/2 in production Chromium, with source text absent from the panel and no filename/content transmission; after the adjacent image and package gates, the complete privacy/offline suite passed 18/18. Complete streaming validity and safety enforcement remains in the conversion worker. Exact scope and cleanup are in `evidence/structured-source-inspection-2026-09-26.json`.",
  "- **2026-09-27 bounded image-source inspection, completed 2026-09-28:** The pre-conversion panel reads at most a fixed 1 MiB binary header and reports dimensions, declared depth/color form, animation/page structure, and bounded metadata signals for PNG/APNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, AVIF, BMP, ICO, and classic TIFF without decoding pixels; SVG uses the 256 KiB UTF-8 structural path. JPEG XL now lazy-loads a separate pinned libjxl 0.12.0 worker with fixed 16 MiB Wasm, 64 KiB reads, a 128 KiB input window, an 8 MiB decoder-allocation limit, a 4 MiB scan ceiling, and a 1,000-frame cap. It subscribes only to basic-information and displayed-frame events, skips frame pixel output, and reports exact or lower-bound frame counts plus timing/loop/name signals. Genuine static RGB, static 16-bit grayscale, eight-frame animated, and corrupt JPEG XL fixtures joined the existing PNG/APNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP/AVIF/BMP/TIFF/SVG gate; the focused production-Chromium test passed 1/1 and the complete privacy/offline suite passed 18/18. Exact scope, hashes, and cleanup are in `evidence/image-source-inspection-2026-09-27.json`.",
  "- **2026-09-28 JPEG XL inspection worker lifecycle extension:** The static worker audit now inventories 32 production TypeScript worker modules and all three message loops. The JPEG XL inspection loop has no mutable module-scoped job state, caps error responses at 2,048 characters, closes itself after its one terminal response, and is also terminated by the caller on success, error, cancellation, or the 30-second deadline. The audit passes within the 248/248 unit suite.",
  "- **2026-09-27 bounded archive/package source inspection:** ZIP, DOCX, XLSX, PPTX, ODT, ODS, ODP, and EPUB read only the 65,557-byte ZIP tail plus at most a 1 MiB central-directory prefix. TAR performs at most 256 isolated 512-byte header reads while skipping payloads; GZIP/BZIP2/XZ wrappers read at most 64 KiB plus an eight-byte GZIP trailer; 7Z reads its 32-byte start header plus one next-header marker. The panel reports bounded entry/size/compression/encryption/path-safety and package facts without decompressing payloads or displaying names. Eight ZIP-based families, TAR/TAR.GZ/TAR.BZ2/TAR.XZ/7Z, raw GZIP/BZIP2/XZ, unsafe ZIP/TAR, an in-memory 20,000-entry oversized ZIP directory, and a 1,024-entry TAR passed the focused production-Chromium gate 1/1. The ZIP stress stopped at exactly 1,114,133 bytes; TAR stopped at exactly 131,072 header bytes and both qualified incomplete counts. Exact scope and cleanup are in `evidence/package-source-inspection-2026-09-27.json`.",
